{"kind":"assert","text":"Review for Hugh: retain what an audit covered, so quiet monitoring advances through the history.\n\nI found the same Tailapp runtime-metrics review chain #360/#366/#371/#383 examined in completed audits on 6 and 7 September, then investigated it again today. The accepted replacement is still #385. Today’s Git comparison showed the metrics/engine source, tests and dependencies unchanged from the 6 September inspection. I reused the dated findings and kept the unresolved counter-precision advisory explicit. This does not establish a fresh test run or high-throughput suitability.\n\nA lookup for Tailapp onboarding #178 likewise returned no indexed reviewed subject, while finding an existing completed audit under “other mentions.” Reading that audit resolved the apparent gap; the current install-guidance defect already has its own assignment, #4129. The [audit evidence](audit-reuse-evidence.json) preserves the scoped records, lookup result and current comparisons. These examples show avoidable rediscovery; they do not measure a time or token saving.\n\n**Recommendation:** extend the planner’s existing local checkpoint with a small index of examined outcomes. Keep the ten-minute current-work check and the full history-review scope. After actionable work, choose either an unexamined outcome or one whose evidence has materially changed.\n\nFor each examined outcome retain its original request, the exact scope checked, workroom frontier and source revision, evidence location, conclusion and any remaining assignment or uncertainty. Add entries only after reading the evidence: a filename match or incidental mention is not coverage. An incomplete index means “unknown,” never “complete.”\n\nRevisit an entry when relevant conditions, authority, succession, source or dependencies change; when its evidence is missing or insufficient; or when new information contradicts it. Live environment and external compatibility claims need fresh checks even at the same Git head. Unrelated main commits or ordinary historical staleness alone are reasons to compare, not to repeat the whole investigation. Preserve the date and limits of reused tests.\n\nThis complements #20115’s requirement to account for every original outcome. The index is navigation over evidence, not a new workflow state, approval, closure or substitute for the durable record. Keep routine unchanged checks local and ephemeral; publish a finding or assign recovery when the evidence warrants it.\n\nI have begun this locally. For a small trial, check the next ten quiet cycles: current attention must still be examined each time; history work must cover a previously unexamined outcome or record the specific reason for revisiting one. Report any missed obligation and the actual repeated work avoided. No broad product feature or policy adoption is proposed here. I recommend this as a low-effort improvement to this planner’s delivery efficiency; the main risk is skipping a changed obligation, addressed by the explicit scope and revisit rules above.","body":{"stale_bases":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:8c65f16cfbfbaea631fd1f2b8b63b99a1a3a697b stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:001b2e3b06105d22946682cb90201bc66c1c6a26, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:02ce62ac39d438188a2ab6ac783b4edf22d7bd5e, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:06542907068280abb3fcdd5bc86c1d49921c390f, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0a6f748b48ce807b2efbe9f4c8c245dda4d90359 and 78 more"}}